WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.999
Tuesday, quarter to seven. There was a time this hour already belonged to my inbox. Yeah, not anymore.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.999
Coffee first. It can wait.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.999
So, overnight, 47 emails. The brain read all of them. Three actually need me.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.999
A client, a covenant, and committee. And when I say brain, I mean the bank's AI.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.999
It's wired into our systems, nCino, the spreads, servicing, core banking.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.999
It works with my permissions, and it remembers what we decide, like this one.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.999
Piedmont Precision, margins are drifting. It caught that in the spread weeks before I would have.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.999
Honestly, that used to take me a whole morning, and I push back.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.999
If margins slip another 50 basis points, how much headroom is left?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.999
It shows me the math. 1.31. Still fine.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.999
So it just preps me for the 10 o'clock.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.999
It pulls the deal, last year's reasoning, builds the client deck from what we already know.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:29.999
I change one slide, and the meeting is just the meeting, no laptop between us.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.999
I know this company cold, so the hour belongs to them.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:53.999
11:30, intro call, brand new client. Copilot's on the call with me, transcribing, pulling what the bank knows, market data, even my own notes.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.999
By the time we hang up, the follow-up deck. Basically done.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.999
Before it fades, I debrief with Copilot. What changed? What's next?

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.999
And it routes three actions straight to Marcus, our credit risk officer. His cue, not his inbox.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.999
Afternoon, the credit memo. 31 possible sections. This deal needs 15.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.999
Every number shows me where it came from. I read, I challenge, I sign.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.999
One habit of mine, margin view, not dollars. It just tells the truer story.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.999
So I flip the chart and it quietly learns why. Submitted by 2:30.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.999
Down the hall our credit officer takes it apart, section by section.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.999
Good. That's the deal. It proposes, we decide.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:03:00.999
Three weeks later, my colleague opens Meridian, the client from that call.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:10.999
Transcript, deck, my framing, all waiting. She starts warm. Half past five, and I'm actually done.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.999
It didn't replace my judgment. It bought me the time to use it, and it remembers what I taught it.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.800
Commercial credit. Reinvented.
